{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"3286e9a6aeeb25e1bf3fcf613be9da3d3270e55d"},"timestamp":1536874723,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEyOTc4NDg2Nw=="},"target":"a79d4c768db9815beddeba4619b1df5693679618c2d2f7620a86b3d0e73ea220","message":"This is another follow up question from:\n\nhttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2814#issue-359426631\n\nOn the Github repository for datalad, I am able to explore many levels into a dataset, e.g.,: http://datasets.datalad.org/?dir=/adhd200/RawDataBIDS/NeuroIMAGE\n\nWhen creating datasets, in that you can create subfolders with their own datasets, at what level is (traditionally) appropriate to initialize? Is the standard to call git init at the root, and then define a datalad dataset at each level? Just the top level?\n\nGiven this decision, what makes it possible to interactively click into all those levels?\n\nIs this website just another remote for the actual dataset and this is possible because you are using a module to browse the file system?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"73651a459f29dd7450c6c7cc39df246c43793a49"},"timestamp":1536874804,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTE2MjE1NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2825#issuecomment-421162154"},"message":"[GitMate.io](https://gitmate.io) thinks possibly related issues are https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1028 (Dataset singletons), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/656 (ADHD200 dataset), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/36 (Mitchell's dataset), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2801 ([question] the \"dataset id\" is meant to be the same for all siblings, correct?), and https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/824 (how to uninstall a dataset???).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536893331,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTIxNDI3NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2825#issuecomment-421214275"},"message":"Again, there is no clear 100% rule we are to follow. But it is similar to code repositories composition.  If something could have a life of its own - better be a separate git repo, and used as a submodule if needed to become a part of a bigger thing.  That is why we have e.g. `openfmri/` and `crcns/` datasets where each subdataset in a separate ... well subdataset ;-)  In aforementioned ADHD200 (silly me for some reason decided to enforce lower case at the first level of the hierarchy there... heh) NeuroIMAGE is one of the \"sites\" which contributed to ADHD200 collection.  So, e.g. if we went to the origin, it would have been created independently from other sites to begin with.\n\nSome times though we need to \"enforce\" a dataset boundary where it might not necessarily be needed to define a thing with a life of its own.  Main reason is -- large number of files.  Eg. in HCP there is tens of thousands of files per each subject.  Git repositories scale to manage only about that much and then things become \"to slow down\". So whenever we create/share HCP dataset (public bucket was finally recreated, we should start crawling the beast!) we will probably have a dataset per each subject.  Might be fun or might be pain - we will discover ;-)\n\n`datalad create` vs `git init`: Current \"understanding\" is to have \"datalad create\" datasets at any level in the hierarchy, i.e. have them git-annex repos, so you could aggregate large amounts of metadata to be kept under git-annex control, instead of blowing up `.git/objects`.  We used to have e.g. `openfmri/` to be a regular git repository, but then made it into annex for that reason.\n\ninteractive:  post-update hook (see e.g. http://datasets.datalad.org/.git/hooks/post-update) gets installed whenever you `--with ui` ;-)  that one calls `datalad ls --json file` which produces file listings metadata dumps (e.g. http://datasets.datalad.org/.git/datalad/metadata/) which are picked up by the JS frontend on the page.  Sorry about confusing/conflicting use of `metadata/` for this purpose -- came about before new and flashy `.datalad/metadata` ;-)","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536893387,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE4NDUwOTYzNDk="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1536904630,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTIzOTcwOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2825#issuecomment-421239708"},"message":"Let me add one more, rather conceptual, aspect. There is no need to decide on a single order/nesting of datasets. Maintaining a hierarchy, such as http://datasets.datalad.org, comes with costs. For example, changes in the nesting (because you feel like grouping by topic, instead of origin) require extensive operations.\n\nIt is important to realize that only direct parent/child (super/subdataset) relationships actually exist, and all other relationships are indirect. Hence it is often more practical to limit subdataset organization to a plain list (pretty much the model Github uses, there is the one list of repos in your account and everthing else is done via some kind of tags).\n\nIf you have subdatasets in a plain list, the URLs through which they are exposed is also unaffected by any semantic/conceptual relationship between datasets (that might change over time).\n\nAt the same time, nothing prevents users from maintaining *additional* topical collections of dataset's, e.g. all datasets from openfmri vs. all datasets on face perception. Also, nothing prevents a dataset from being part in more than one of those collections (at the same or different versions).","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1536904637,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE4NDUyNjIwNDU="},"added":["documentation"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"3286e9a6aeeb25e1bf3fcf613be9da3d3270e55d"},"timestamp":1536931212,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTM1NjAzMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2825#issuecomment-421356033"},"message":"Oh I didn't know about [git hooks](https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Customizing-Git-Git-Hooks) this is super cool! +1 to what you said above, and the general idea of not enforcing some strict thing beyond pretty high level `parent/child`. Closing.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"3286e9a6aeeb25e1bf3fcf613be9da3d3270e55d"},"timestamp":1536931212,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTg0NjA5MjI2MA=="},"status":2}]}